    "\n  body { margin: 0; background: #111; font-family: Inter, sans-serif; }\n  #panel { position: relative; width: 400px; height: 200px; background: #222; }\n  <local-path> A fit-to-viewport wrapper: the idiom that makes Chrome report a\n     post-transform box beside an untransformed font-size. */\n  #shrunk { position: absolute; left: 20px; top: 20px; width: 300px;\n            transform: scale(0.5); transform-origin: 0 0; }\n  #inside { color: #fff; font-size: 40px; letter-spacing: 2px; }\n  <local-path> Same string, same size, outside the wrapper — the control. */\n  #outside { position: absolute; left: 20px; top: 120px; color: #fff;\n             font-size: 40px; letter-spacing: 2px; }\n  <local-path> Two axes: type cannot carry it, so lowering must refuse. */\n  #squashed { position: absolute; left: 20px; top: 160px;\n              transform: scale(0.5, 1.5); transform-origin: 0 0; }\n  #squashed span { color: #fff; font-size: 20px; }\n",
